Certainly, here are some additional steps to set up the Spring BootPlugin, configure the test environment for your Invoice Management application, and mock Oracle EBS using Spring Boot Data JPA:

Setting up Spring BootPlugin:

1. Follow the installation guide for Gauge, which will guide you through the process of setting up the Java plugin.

2. Clone the Gauge Spring BootPlugin repository by running the following command in your terminal:

   ```
   git clone https://github.com/aufs/gauge-spring-boot-plugin.git
   ```

3. In your Gauge project directory, create a new directory named `spring-boot-plugin` and navigate inside it:

   ```
   cd <your-gauge-project-directory>
   mkdir spring-boot-plugin
   cd spring-boot-plugin
   ```

4. In the `spring-boot-plugin` directory, clone the Spring BootPlugin repository:

   ```
   git clone https://github.com/aufs/gauge-spring-boot-plugin.git
   ```

   This will clone the repository inside a subdirectory called `gauge-spring-boot-plugin`.

5. Move the `gauge-spring-boot-plugin` directory to the root of your Gauge project by running these commands:

   ```
   mv gauge-spring-boot-plugin ./
   rm -rf gauge-spring-boot-plugin
   ```

6. In your Gauge project's `grcov.config` file, add the following line to exclude the Spring BootPlugin dependencies from analysis:

   ```
   exclude=["spring-boot-plugin/**"]
   ```

7. Following the configuration instructions in Gauge, add the following lines to your `features.json` configuration file, which will enable the Spring BootPlugin and specify the base directory of your Spring Boot project:

   ```
   {
     "ids": ["*"],
     "dependencies": {
       "gauge-spring-boot-plugin": {
         "source": "local"
       }
     },
     "executors": {
       "maven": {
         "options": ["-s", "./settings.xml", "-P", "integration-test", "clean", "package"]
       }
     },
     "spring": {
       "maven-base-url": "file://$WORKSPACE/",
       "boot-classname": "org.springframework.boot.loader.PropertiesLauncher"
     }
   }
   ```

   The `boot-classname` specifies the class to use as the launch class for your Spring Boot application.

Configuring the Test Environment:

1. Add the following dependencies to your `pom.xml` file to use Spring Boot and Spring Data JPA:

   ```xml
   <dependency>
     <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
     <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
   </dependency>
   <dependency>
     <groupId>com.oracle.database.jdbc</groupId>
     <artifactId>ojdbc8</artifactId>
     <scope>runtime</scope>
   </dependency>
   ```

2. Define a configuration class for your Oracle EBS database connection. You can name this class something like `DatabaseConfig.java`, and you may need to modify the following code to match your specific database credentials:

   ```java
   import java.util.Properties;

   import javax.persistence.EntityManagerFactory;
   import javax.persistence.Persistence;

   import org.hibernate.cfg.AvailableSettings;
   import org.hibernate.cfg.Environment;
   import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
   import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
   import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

   @Configuration
   public class DatabaseConfig {

     @Value("${spring.datasource.url}")
     private String databaseUrl;

     @Value("${spring.datasource.username}")
     private String databaseUsername;

     @Value("${spring.datasource.password}")
     private String databasePassword;

     @Bean
     public EntityManagerFactory entityManagerFactory() {
       Properties props = new Properties();
       props.setProperty(Environment.DRIVER, "oracle.jdbc.driver.OracleDriver");
       props.setProperty(Environment.URL, databaseUrl);
       props.setProperty(Environment.USER, databaseUsername);
       props.setProperty(Environment.PASS, databasePassword);
       props.setProperty(AvailableSettings CURRENT_SESSION_CONTEXT_CLASS, "thread");
       props.setProperty(AvailableSettings CONNECTION_CLOSE, "on");
       props.setProperty(AvailableSettings CONNECTION_RELEASE_MODE, "after_transaction");

       return Persistence.createEntityManagerFactory("test", props);
     }
   }
   ```

   Ensure that the `application.properties` file contains the necessary database connection information:

   ```properties
   spring.datasource.url=jdbc:oracle:thin:@localhost:1521/orclpdb
   spring.datasource.username=your_username
   spring.datasource.password=your_password
   spring.datasource.driver-class-name=oracle.jdbc.driver.OracleDriver
   spring.datasource.tomcat.connection-properties=cacheServerCollections=true
   spring.datasource.testWhileIdle=true
   spring.datasource.validationQuery=SELECT 1 FROM DUAL
   ```

Mocking Oracle EBS:

1. Annotate your test class with the `@ExtendWith(SpringExtension.class)` annotation to enable Spring Boot integration testing.

2. Define a DAO (Data Access Object) interface and its implementation, which will connect to the database or mock service. You can generate the DAO using the `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto` property in your `application.properties` file by setting it to `update` or `create`. Once the database schema is created, you can remove this property.

3. Define a test repository class that extends, say, `CrudRepository` or `JpaRepository`, which will work with your DAO and return mock data when necessary.

4. Use the `@MockBean` or `@SpyBean` annotation from Spring to create mocks of the Oracle EBS services that your Invoice Management application interacts with.

5. Inject the test repository into your test methods and use it to perform your assertions.

