Certainly! Here are some code examples using A-Frame and Three.js for importing a .glb file and accessing its animation names:

## A-Frame Example

First, you'll need to add the A-Frame library to your HTML file:

```html
<html>
  <head>
    <title>An A-Frame GLTF Example</title>
    <script src="https://aframe.io/releases/1.3.0/aframe.min.js"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a-scene>
      <!-- Add a <a-entity> element below for your GLTF model -->
    </a-scene>
    <script>
      // Code here will execute when the page loads
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

Assuming that you have uploaded your .glb file to a server and have the URL ready, you can use the following code to import the model and access its animation names:

```html
<a-scene>
  <a-entity gltf-model="#my-model" animation-attachments="#my-model" id="model"></a-entity>
  <script>
    // Add a model to the scene with ID="model"
    const model = document.querySelector('#model');

    // Load the GLTF model from the server with the given URL
    const modelUrl = 'https://your-server-url.com/your-glb-model.glb';

    // After the model is loaded, access its animation names
    model.addEventListener('model-loaded', () => {
      const animations = model.object3D.traverse((child) => {
        if (child.animations && child.animations.length > 0) {
          return child.animations;
        }
      });

      console.log(animations);
    });

    // Include a dynamic <script> element to load the GLTF model
    const script = document.createElement('script');
    script.src = modelUrl;
    document.body.appendChild(script);
  </script>
</a-scene>
```

In this example, the `gltf-model` property specifies the ID of a `<script>` element (prefixed with "#") that contains the GLTF model. The `animation-attachments` property specifies the same ID, so that A-Frame can access the animation data from the GLTF model.

After the model has loaded, you can traverse the `.object3D` object hierarchy to access the animations using the `traverse()` method. If an animation is detected, its `animations` property will be returned.

## Three.js Example

First, add the Three.js library to your HTML file:

```html
<html>
  <head>
    <title>A Three.js GLTF Example</title>
    <script src="https://threejs.org/build/three.min.js"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <script>
      // Code here will execute when the page loads
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

Similarly, you'll need to upload your .glb file to a server and have the URL ready.

```html
<script>
  // Load the GLTF model from the server with the given URL
  const modelUrl = 'https://your-server-url.com/your-glb-model.glb';

  // Load the Three.js GLTF loader
  const gltfLoader = new THREE.GLTFLoader();

  // Create a `Scene` and a `SceneLoader` for loading the model
  const scene = new THREE.Scene();
  const sceneLoader = new THREE.SceneLoader();

  // Include the loaded GLTF model in the scene
  sceneLoader.parse(modelUrl, (gltf) => {
    // Access the animations from the GLTF model
    const animations = gltf.scene.animations;

    console.log(animations);

    // Combine the GLTF model with the Three.js scene
    scene.mergeSpheres(gltf.scene);
  });

  // Create and add to the WebGL renderer
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ canvas });

  document.body.appendChild(canvas);
</script>
```

In this example, the `THREE.GLTFLoader` class is used to load and parse the .glb file. The `parse()` method returns a GLTF object, which has a `scene` property containing the loaded GLTF model. The `animations` property contains the animation data.

After the model has loaded, you can combine it with your Three.js `Scene`, and then create and add a renderer to render the scene onto a canvas element.

I hope these examples help you get started with importing and accessing animations from a .glb file using A-Frame and Three.js!

