Sure, I'd be happy to provide you with more information on adding other routes and middleware to your Express.js server!

## Adding Routes

In Express.js, you define routes by registering route handlers using the `app.<HTTP_METHOD>(URL_PATH, callback)` method (`HTTP_METHOD` could be `get`, `post`, `put`, `delete`, etc.). The callback function (or a series of middleware functions) will be executed when the server receives a request for that URL path with the specified HTTP method.

Here is an example of how to define additional routes:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// existing route
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello, World!');
});

// define a new route
app.get('/about', (req, res) => {
  res.send('This is the About page');
});

// define a route with a parameter
app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  const userId = req.params.id;
  res.send(`User ${userId} details`);
});

// start server listening
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server listening on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

In this example, we've added two new routes: one that responds with "This is the About page" when accessed with the path `/about`, and another that expects a parameter (`:id`) as part of the URL. When accessed, it will respond with a message that includes the value of the `:id` parameter.

## Adding Middleware

Middleware functions can be used to enhance functionality in your Express.js application. They are functions that take four arguments (including the `req`, `res`, and `next` arguments) and allow you to execute code at specific points of the HTTP request-response cycle.

By using middleware, you can perform actions such as logging requests, validating user input, and handling errors.

Here's an example of how to implement middleware:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// using a built-in middleware
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));

// custom middleware function
function logRequest(req, res, next) {
  console.log(`${req.method} request for ${req.originalUrl}`);
  next();
}

// define a route with middleware
app.get('/users/:id', logRequest, (req, res) => {
  const userId = req.params.id;
  res.send(`User ${userId} details`);
});

// start server listening
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server listening on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

In this example, we've added two pieces of middleware:

- `express.urlencoded({ extended: true })`: a built-in middleware function provided by Express.js that parses incoming request data in a middleware before your handlers, available with the `express` package. In this case, we've enabled the support of URL-encoded bodies.
- `logRequest`: a custom middleware function that logs a message every time a request is made, executed before the route handler function.

When you define middleware, you can chain them together by passing the subsequent middleware function as an argument:

```javascript
function validateInput(req, res, next) {
  if (req.body.username && req.body.email) {
    next();
  } else {
    res.status(400).send('Username and email are required.');
  }
}

app.post('/register', validateInput, (req, res) => {
  // register a new user with the provided username and email
});
```

You can call middleware functions multiple times by invoking `app.use()`:

```javascript
function firstMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  console.log('First middleware');
  next();
}

function secondMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  console.log('Second middleware');
  next();
}

function fourthMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  console.log('Fourth middleware');
  next();
}

app.use(firstMiddleware);
app.use(secondMiddleware);
app.use(fourthMiddleware);

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello, World!');
});
```

This will execute all middleware functions when an HTTP request is made to the root route (`GET /`).

