\usepackage{amsmath, amssymb, booktabs} % Useful math and tabular packages

\begin{document}

\textbf{Настраиваемые комбинации символов}

LaTeX provides a vast number of predefined mathematical symbols, some of which are listed in the \href{https://www.rpi.edu/dept/arc/wiki/latex_math_symbols}{RPI LaTeX Math Symbols} wiki. However, some symbols are not included, and in such cases, we'll need to define them ourselves.

\begin{tabular}{>{\raggedright}p{0.3\linewidth}>{\centering}p{0.55\linewidth}}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Symbol} & \textbf{Definition} \\
    \midrule
    $\mathcal{A}$ & \verb|\mathcal{A}| \\
    $\mathbb{R}$ & \verb|\mathbb{R}| \\
    $\Re$ & \verb|\Re| \\
    $\Im$ & \verb|\Im| \\
    $\infty$ & \verb|\infty| \\
    \bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\textbf{Математические уравнения}
LaTeX provides powerful tools to write math equations. Here are some essential packages: \texttt{amsmath}, \texttt{amssymb}, and \texttt{mathtools}.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \verb|\( ... \)| for inline math
    \item \verb|\[ ... \]| for display math equations
    \item \verb|\begin{align} ... \end{align}| to align equations left to right
    \item \verb|\begin{flalign} ... \end{flalign}| to align equations with & character
\end{itemize}

Basic math operators:

\begin{center}
    \begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
        \toprule
        \textbf{Operator} & \textbf{Syntax} \\
        \midrule
        Summation & \verb|\sum_{lower}^{upper}| \\
        Product & \verb|\prod_{lower}^{upper}| \\
        Integral & \verb|\int_{lower}^{upper}|
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
\end{center}

Big operators (\verb|\bigcap|, \verb|\bigodot|, etc.) can be used to adjust the size of operators in a formula.

\begin{align*}
    f(x) &= A \cdot g(x) + B \cdot h(x) \\
    \max\_{i=1,\dots,n} x\_i &= \underset{i=1,\dots,n}{\operatorname*{arg\,max}}\, x\_i \\
