\title{Mathematical Modeling of Infectious Disease Spread}

\author{Your Name}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\section*{Introduction}

Infectious diseases have plagued humanity for centuries, and their continued presence and emergence pose a significant threat to global health. Mathematical modeling provides a powerful tool to study the spread of infectious diseases and develop effective control strategies. This presentation highlights three key skills and achievements in the field, which are modeling of the SIR (Susceptible-Infected-Recovered) epidemic, simulation using an epidemic SEIR (Susceptible-Exposed-Infected-Recovered) model, and evaluation of vaccination strategies.

\newpage

\section*{Modeling the SIR Epidemic}

The SIR model describes the spread of an infectious disease through a population subdivided into three compartments: susceptible (S), infected (I), and recovered (R) individuals. The rate equations for SIR model are:

\begin{alignat*}{2}
\frac{dS}{dt} &= - \frac{\beta IS}{N}, \\
\frac{dI}{dt} &= \frac{\beta IS}{N} - \alpha I, \\
\frac{dR}{dt} &= \alpha I,
\end{alignat*}

where $\beta$ is the transmission coefficient that determines the infection rate, $\alpha$ is the recovery rate or inverse of the mean infectious period, and $N=S+I+R$ is the total population size.

The SIR model provides important insights into the basic reproductive number $R_0$ which describes the expected number of secondary infections generated by a single infected individual in a fully susceptible population. By solving the model at the early exponential growth phase, we obtain:

$$
R_0 = \frac{\beta}{\alpha}.
$$

In the long-term, the disease eventually disappears due to the depletion of susceptible individuals. The endpoint of the SIR model is:

$$
\lim_{t \to \infty} \begin{pmatrix}
S \\
I \\
R \\
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
N - I(0) \\
I(0) \\
\sum_{t=0}^\infty I(t) /\alpha \\
\end{pmatrix},
$$

where $I(0)$ is the initial number of infectious individuals.

\newpage

\section*{Simulation using SEIR Epidemic Model}

The SEIR model extends the SIR model by incorporating an exposed (E) compartment, which describes individuals who were infected but are not yet infectious. The rate equations for the SEIR model are:

\begin{alignat*}{2}
\frac{dS}{dt} & = - \frac{\beta I E}{N}, \\
\frac{dE}{dt} & = \frac{\beta I S}{N} - \sigma E, \\
\frac{dI}{dt} & = \sigma E - \alpha I, \\
\frac{dR}{dt} & = \alpha I,
\end{alignat*}

where $\sigma$ is the rate at which exposed individuals progress to the infectious stage.

The SEIR model better captures the disease dynamics, especially for infectious diseases with a latent period, such as COVID-19 or tuberculosis. Using appropriate parameter values and initial conditions, the SEIR model enables us to simulate and visualize the spread of an infectious disease.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{SEIR_simulation.pdf}
\caption{SEIR simulation for COVID-19 in the United States. (Data source: Johns Hopkins University)}
\label{fig:SEIR_sim}
\end{figure}

\newpage

\section*{Evaluation of Vaccination Strategies}

Vaccination has played a critical role in controlling and eradicating infectious diseases. To analyze the effectiveness of vaccination strategies, one can modify the SEIR model and include the vaccinated (V) compartment.

The extended SEIRV model considers the following rate equations:

\begin{alignat*}{2}
\frac{dS}{dt} & = - \frac{\beta V E}{N} - \mu S, \\
\frac{dE}{dt} & = \frac{\beta V S}{N} + \frac{\beta I S}{N} - (\sigma + \mu) E, \\
\frac{dI}{dt} & = \sigma E - (\alpha + \mu) I, \\
\frac{dR}{dt} & = \alpha I - \mu R, \\
\frac{dV}{dt} & = \kappa - \mu V,
\end{alignat*}

Here, $\mu$ is the disease-independent mortality rate, and $\kappa$ represents the rate at which susceptible individuals get vaccinated; we assume that vaccinated individuals are not initially infectious.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{SEIRV_vaccination.pdf}
\caption{Effectiveness of vaccination strategies in reducing the basic reproduction number $R_0$.}
\label{fig:SEIRV_vacc}
\end{figure}

By \textit{forward} and \textit{backward} sensitivity analysis, we can quantitatively evaluate the effectiveness of vaccination strategies, depending on the vaccine deployment pace and coverage percentage.

\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial R_0}{\partial \kappa} > 0, \qquad \frac{\partial^2 R_0}{\partial \kappa^2} < 0, \\
\frac{\partial R_0}{\partial (\% \ \textrm{vaccinated})} < 0.
\end{align*}

These results demonstrate that augmenting vaccination efforts can markedly reduce the disease transmission and, hence, help control the outbreak.

\newpage

\section*{Conclusion}

Mathematical modeling has proved valuable in gaining a qualitative and quantitative understanding of infectious disease spread, helping to inform disease control measures. Through the SIR and SEIR models, we have established a foundation for modeling epidemic dynamics, and the SEIRV model showcased the effectiveness of 

%\begin{flushleft}
%...
%\end{flushleft}

vaccination strategies. In the face of emerging infectious diseases or evolving disease transmission patterns, tailored modeling efforts can aid public health decision-making and ultimately save lives.

