Certainly! Here are some examples to illustrate how this rule works in practice:

## Examples

### Passing data to a non-state-changing method:

Correct:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <button @click="onClick(1)">Click Me</button>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  methods: {
    onClick(value) {
      console.log(value)
    },
    created() {},
    mounted() {}
  }
}
</script>
```

In this example, `onClick` is passed a non-state-changing value, `1`, so calling it from a lifecycle hook doesn't violate the rule.

### Modifying internal state directly:

Incorrect:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <button @click="onClick()">Click Me</button>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  data() {
    return { count: 0 }
  },
  methods: {
    onClick() {
      this.count += 1 // modifies internal state
    },
    created() {},
    mounted() {}
  }
}
</script>
```

In this example, `onClick` modifies the internal state of the component (`count += 1`), so calling it directly from a lifecycle hook would violate the rule.

### Invoking a state-modifying method:

Incorrect:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <button @click="onClick()">Click Me</button>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  data() {
    return { items: [] }
  },
  methods: {
    addItem() {
      this.items.push(1) // modifies internal state
    },
    onClick() {
      this.addItem() // invokes a state-modifying method
    },
    created() {},
    mounted() {}
  }
}
</script>
```

In this example, `addItem` modifies the internal state of the component (`this.items.push(1)`), so invoking it from `onClick` would violate the rule.

## Excluding specific methods/components

To exclude specific methods or components from being checked by this rule, you can add them to a special comment block in the script section of the component. 

### Excluding specific methods:

To exclude specific methods, add a comment block to the top of each method, like this:

```vue
<template>
  <!-- ... -->
</template>

<script>
export default {
  // ...
  methods: {
    // state-changing method
    updateCount() {
      this.count += 1
    },
    // Do not lint this method!
    doNotLint() {
      // this will not be checked
    },
    // do something else without modifying state
    dontModifyState() {},
  },
  // ...
}
</script>
```

### Excluding components:

To exclude all methods in a component, add a comment block directly to the component, like this:

```vue
<template>
  <!-- ... -->
</template>

<script>
export default {
  // Do not lint this component
  /* eslint-disable comprehensible-vue/stateless-hooks */
  // ...
}
</script>
```

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <!-- ... -->
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  // Do not lint any methods in this component
  /* eslint-disable comprehensible-vue/stateless-hooks */
  data() {},
  methods: {
    // state-changing method
    updateCount() {
      this.count += 1
    },
    // other method
    doSomething() {},
  },
  // ...
}
</script>
```

These comments will disable the specific rule using ESLint's inline configuration.

Note that these comments will disable the entire rule for the excluded items, and not just the specific state-changing modifications. If you have methods that only make state-changing modifications, consider splitting them out into their own single-purpose components.

